-- title:
--   Infinitesimal points push forward along algebra maps
-- statement:
--   Let $B$ be a commutative ring, let $A$ be a scheme and let $f \colon A \to \operatorname{Spec} B$ be a morphism carrying a relative group law $L$, that is, a choice of unit, multiplication and inverse on the sets $\{\varphi \colon T \to A \mid \varphi \text{ followed by } f = t\}$ of $A$-points over each $t \colon T \to \operatorname{Spec} B$, satisfying the group axioms and naturality in $(T,t)$. Let $D$ and $C$ be commutative $B$-algebras, let $\varphi \colon D \to C$ be a $B$-algebra homomorphism, let $J \subseteq D$ be an ideal, and let $Q$ be a morphism $\operatorname{Spec} D \to A$ whose composite with $f$ is $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the structure map $B \to D$. Assume $Q$ is infinitesimal modulo $J$, i.e. precomposing $Q$ with $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the quotient map $D \to D/J$ yields exactly the unit point $L.\mathrm{one}$ of $\operatorname{Spec}(D/J)$ over $\operatorname{Spec} B$. Then the point of $A$ obtained by precomposing $Q$ with $\operatorname{Spec} \varphi$, viewed over the structure map of $C$, is infinitesimal modulo the extended ideal $J.\mathrm{map}\,\varphi$, the ideal of $C$ generated by $\varphi(J)$.
--
--   This records that the condition of reducing to the unit section modulo an ideal is preserved under base change of the test algebra, the extended ideal replacing the original one. It is used in the construction of infinitesimal (formal-group) neighbourhoods of the unit section, where a point defined over a particular finite algebra must be transported to an arbitrary test algebra, as in [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.FakeEllipticCurve.exists_isNilpotent_isInfinitesimal_of_nsmulPt_pow_eq_one_of_one_mem`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.FakeEllipticCurve.exists_isNilpotent_isInfinitesimal_of_nsmulPt_pow_eq_one_of_one_mem).
